<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 0.9em; margin-top: 5px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> I Like School!</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Like Math!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Express likes or dislikes using simple words and pointing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can identify things in simple pictures, using isolated words (e.g., 'art' or 'games'). Can express likes or dislikes to peers using simple words and pointing (e.g., 'I like music!' while pointing to a picture). Can respond to simple questions like 'Do you like it?' with 'yes' or 'no' and gestures (e.g., nodding).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification. Activating prior knowledge and introducing the target language for expressing likes and dislikes.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "What do you like?" song and actions. Students stand up and point to large flashcards of school subjects (math, art, music, playground, science, English). The teacher models, "I like math!" while pointing, and students repeat chorally. This revisits vocabulary from previous lessons.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard for 'math' with a happy face and says, "I like math!" (emphasizing /l/ sound). Then, the teacher holds up a flashcard for a 'boring' subject with a sad face and says, "I don't like (it)." The teacher uses clear gestures and facial expressions. Students observe.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a happy face: "When I say 'I like', am I happy?" (Students nod 'yes').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a sad face: "When I say 'I don't like', am I happy?" (Students shake head 'no').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'math' a school subject?" (Students nod 'yes').</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to the Scenario "I Like School!". Presenting expressions for likes/dislikes in context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An illustrated story sequence (e.g., a simple picture book or large laminated cards) featuring children in different school settings (playground, art class, music class, math class). Each picture shows a child expressing either 'I like...' or 'I don't like...' with a speech bubble and a clear emotional expression. For example, a child happily counting blocks for 'math' ("I like math!"). A child drawing with colorful crayons ("I like art!"). Another child looking bored with a book ("I don't like reading!").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Expressing likes and dislikes using simple phrases. Understanding and responding to basic preference questions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows each picture, narrates briefly, and points to the speech bubble. "Look! The boy is at the playground. He says, 'I like playground!'" (emphasizing /g/ in playground).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Point to the child who likes music." (Students point).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does the girl like art? (Teacher asks, students nod 'yes' or shake head 'no')."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Show me happy face for 'I like' and sad face for 'I don't like'." (Students use facial expressions).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking. Practicing target grammar and vocabulary in controlled and semi-controlled activities.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Subject + verb ('I like school.'), Present simple ('I like games.'), Yes/No questions ('Do you like it?' 'Yes.').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> nouns: teacher, art, playground, science, music, math, English; verbs: enjoy, count, color; adjectives: interesting, funny, exciting, happy.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> /l/ in 'like', /g/ in 'games'; phonemic awareness of 'math' and 'games' sounds.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic:</b> Expressing likes and dislikes. Answering preference questions.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Choral Repetition with Flashcards:</b> The teacher shows a large flashcard of 'math' and says, "I like math!" Students repeat chorally, "I like math!" The teacher then shows 'art' and says, "I like art!" (Students repeat). The teacher then shows a less popular subject (e.g., 'boring' science) and says, "I don't like science." Students repeat, "I don't like science." The teacher focuses on clear pronunciation of /l/ in 'like' and /g/ in 'games' / 'playground'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. "Yes/No, I Like It!" Game:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard (e.g., 'music'). Teacher asks, "Do you like music?" Students respond chorally with a loud "Yes!" and a thumbs up, or "No!" and a thumbs down, according to their preference, then try to say "I like music!" or "I don't like music!". The teacher guides them to use gestures (nodding/shaking head) with their responses. This includes practicing 'math' and 'games' for phonemic awareness.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Preference Share:</b> Students are given small flashcards (e.g., one with 'math', one with 'art', one with 'playground'). In pairs, one student shows a flashcard and says, "I like math!" or "I don't like art!". The other student responds, "Me too!" (if they like it) or "Oh, I don't!" (if they dislike it), trying to say "I like math!" or "I don't like art!". The teacher encourages them to use simple adjectives like 'colorful' art or 'funny' games.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario "I Like School!". Focus on fluency, collaborative interaction, and task completion.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "School Subject Interview" – a simple, guided interview activity where students interact with peers to share preferences about school subjects.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each student receives a simple picture card representing a school subject (e.g., 'math', 'art', 'music', 'playground').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher models the activity: "Hello, (Student's Name)! Do you like math?" (Teacher points to 'math' card). Student responds: "Yes!" (with a nod) or "No!" (with a shake). Then, "I like math!" or "I don't like math!".</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students stand up and circulate around the classroom. They approach a peer, show their picture card, and ask, "Do you like (subject on card)?" (e.g., "Do you like art?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The peer responds with "Yes, I do!" (and a thumbs up/nod) or "No, I don't!" (and a thumbs down/shake), then tries to add "I like (art)!" or "I don't like (art)!".</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After asking and answering one question, students switch roles or move to a new peer. The teacher encourages students to use vocabulary like 'enjoy', 'create', 'count', and adjectives like 'interesting', 'exciting' if they are able.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, providing support and prompting when needed, ensuring focus on /l/ and /g/ sounds.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy to check understanding and speaking ability, followed by student self-assessment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> During the "School Subject Interview" (Stage 4), the teacher observes individual students' ability to express likes/dislikes and respond to questions. The teacher will make quick notes on a simple checklist for a few target students.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of /l/ in 'like' and /g/ in 'games'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., 'math', 'art', 'music', 'playground').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to use 'I like...' and 'I don't like...' to express preferences.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to respond to 'Do you like it?' with 'yes' or 'no' and gestures.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and interaction with peers.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a picture of a child saying "I like math!" with a happy face. "Show me your happy face if you can say 'I like math!' today."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a picture of a child thinking. "Put your finger on your head if you still need help saying 'I don't like...'."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Opportunity for students and teacher to reflect on the lesson's effectiveness and plan for future learning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite school subject to talk about today? Why?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or a little hard to ask your friends 'Do you like it?'"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this speaking-focused lesson? (e.g., TPR, visual aids, peer interaction, repetition of 'I like math!' and 'I don't like games!').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students to confidently express likes/dislikes? (e.g., more direct modeling for "I don't like...", more opportunities for one-on-one interaction).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can the pronunciation of /l/ and /g/, and the phonemic awareness of 'math' and 'games' be further reinforced?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing), students will practice writing sentences about what they like and don't like about school, building on today's speaking practice by translating their oral expressions into simple written forms.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>